From: FlipperOneEmu
Subject: [PATCH] target/arm: Linux's KPTI flips of TTBR1_EL1 keep the TLB

With KASLR, Linux forces KPTI on cores without E0PD, as the RK3576's
Cortex-A72s and A53s are, and U-Boot gives the kernel a seed for it, as
on the device. KPTI flips TTBR1_EL1 at every entry to the kernel from
EL0 and every return to EL0: the kernel's tables with an mm's even
ASID, the trampoline's with the odd one. QEMU's TLB has no ASIDs, so
each change of the ASID flushed it whole, and the TB jump cache with
it, twice for every system call and interrupt from EL0: Flipper OS took
167-174 s to its login with KASLR, 91-111 s without.

Both ASIDs of a flip are the same mm: TTBR0's mappings are the same
under either, the trampoline is mapped alike in both tables, and Linux
invalidates each change of a mapping with its TLBI, which QEMU applies
for every ASID. So with the CPU's new x-kpti-asid-pairs, a write to
TTBR1_EL1 that changes the ASID's bit 0 alone, and the tables' address
with it, keeps the TLB. Without KPTI an ASID and the next may be two
mms, but switching between them leaves TTBR1's tables where they are,
so that still flushes, as does every other change of the ASID.

---
--- a/target/arm/cpu.h	2026-11-26 18:17:00.274874428 -0500
+++ b/target/arm/cpu.h	2026-11-26 18:17:44.447079561 -0500
@@ -1133,6 +1133,12 @@
     /* QOM property to indicate we should use the back-compat QARMA5 default */
     bool backcompat_pauth_default_use_qarma5;
 
+    /*
+     * QOM property: TTBR1_EL1's KPTI flips (Linux's: one mm's pair of
+     * ASIDs, the kernel's tables and the trampoline's) keep the TLB
+     */
+    bool kpti_asid_pairs;
+
     /* Specify the number of cores in this CPU cluster. Used for the L2CTLR
      * register.
      */
--- a/target/arm/cpu.c	2026-11-26 18:17:00.277201536 -0500
+++ b/target/arm/cpu.c	2026-11-26 18:17:44.447659975 -0500
@@ -2506,6 +2506,8 @@
     DEFINE_PROP_BOOL("backcompat-cntfrq", ARMCPU, backcompat_cntfrq, false),
     DEFINE_PROP_BOOL("backcompat-pauth-default-use-qarma5", ARMCPU,
                       backcompat_pauth_default_use_qarma5, false),
+    /* True for TTBR1_EL1's KPTI flips to keep the TLB (vmsa_ttbr_write) */
+    DEFINE_PROP_BOOL("x-kpti-asid-pairs", ARMCPU, kpti_asid_pairs, false),
 };
 
 static const gchar *arm_gdb_arch_name(CPUState *cs)
--- a/target/arm/helper.c	2026-11-26 18:17:00.281494496 -0500
+++ b/target/arm/helper.c	2026-11-26 18:17:44.448895418 -0500
@@ -2818,12 +2818,37 @@
     raw_write(env, ri, value);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Linux's KPTI (kernel page table isolation): at each entry to the kernel
+ * from EL0 and each return to EL0, TTBR1_EL1 flips between the kernel's
+ * tables with an mm's ASID, even, and the trampoline's tables with the
+ * same ASID and its bit 0 set (USER_ASID_FLAG). Both are the same mm:
+ * TTBR0's mappings are the same under either, the trampoline is mapped
+ * alike in both tables, and every change of a mapping comes with its TLBI
+ * (for every ASID, here). So such a flip need not flush the TLB, which
+ * otherwise empties twice for every system call and interrupt from EL0.
+ * Only so with the CPU's x-kpti-asid-pairs, and only where the tables'
+ * address changes too: without KPTI an ASID and the next one may well be
+ * two mms, but their switch keeps TTBR1's tables.
+ */
+static bool vmsa_ttbr_kpti_flip(CPUARMState *env, const ARMCPRegInfo *ri,
+                                uint64_t value)
+{
+    uint64_t diff = raw_read(env, ri) ^ value;
+
+    return env_archcpu(env)->kpti_asid_pairs &&
+           ri->state == ARM_CP_STATE_AA64 &&
+           ri->fieldoffset == offsetof(CPUARMState, cp15.ttbr1_el[1]) &&
+           extract64(diff, 48, 16) == 1 && extract64(diff, 1, 47) != 0;
+}
+
 static void vmsa_ttbr_write(CPUARMState *env, const ARMCPRegInfo *ri,
                             uint64_t value)
 {
     /* If the ASID changes (with a 64-bit write), we must flush the TLB.  */
     if (cpreg_field_type(ri) == MO_64 &&
-        extract64(raw_read(env, ri) ^ value, 48, 16) != 0) {
+        extract64(raw_read(env, ri) ^ value, 48, 16) != 0 &&
+        !vmsa_ttbr_kpti_flip(env, ri, value)) {
         ARMCPU *cpu = env_archcpu(env);
         tlb_flush(CPU(cpu));
     }
